# soc-mcp-server — only the settings this service reads.
#
# Split out of the SOC-AI monorepo, where one .env was shared by every
# container. Sharing it meant the Splunk connector's environment also held
# the database password and the ThreatQ credentials, so a compromise of any
# one service read the secrets of all of them.

# ========== DATABASE ==========

# Prisma (NestJS core backend) — plain postgresql:// scheme

# SQLAlchemy (Python AI service) — asyncpg scheme

# ========== REDIS ==========

# ========== QUEUE / WORKER ==========
# APP_ROLE is set per container in docker-compose.yml (api | worker)

# ========== AI SERVICE (FastAPI) ==========
# Seconds the backend waits on soc-ai. Covers the whole agent chain
# (soc-ai -> LLM server -> analysing agent -> query agent -> Splunk),
# so keep it above LLM_TIMEOUT below. CPU-only inference needs minutes.

# ========== MCP CONNECTORS ==========
# soc-mcp-splunk refuses to start unless the three below are set — an unset
# token would mean an open proxy into Splunk for anything on soc-network.
# See mcp/splunk/README.md for the full table.
# ---------- soc-mcp-splunk ----------
# Bearer token soc-ai presents to the MCP server. Generate: openssl rand -hex 32
MCP_AUTH_TOKEN=
# Splunk auth token for a SEARCH-ONLY role, scoped to the indexes below.
# Use a token, never a username and password.
SPLUNK_TOKEN=
# Comma-separated index allowlist every search is confined to.
# "*" disables the restriction and has to be typed out on purpose.
# The data catalog: what each index and sourcetype holds and which field
# carries which meaning. Written by a person from GET /catalog output.
# Unset = CIM field names, which are right only on a normalised estate.
SPLUNK_CATALOG_FILE=/catalog/splunk-catalog.json
SPLUNK_ALLOWED_INDEXES=
# Management port, not the web UI on 8000.
SPLUNK_BASE_URL=https://splunk:8089
# Set false only for a self-signed lab cert.
SPLUNK_VERIFY_SSL=true
SPLUNK_TIMEOUT=60
# Hard ceiling on rows returned to the model, whatever it asks for.
SPLUNK_MAX_COUNT=200
# Splunk ES defaults; a plain Splunk install differs.
SPLUNK_ALERT_INDEX=notable
SPLUNK_ALERT_NAME_FIELD=search_name
MCP_PORT=8200
MCP_ALLOWED_HOSTS=soc-mcp-splunk:8200,localhost:8200,127.0.0.1:8200

# ---------- soc-mcp-threatq ----------
# Gateway in front of ThreatQ's own MCP / agent services. Behind the "threatq"
# compose profile, so it stays out of the default `docker compose up`.
# See mcp/threatq/README.md.
# Bearer token soc-ai presents. Its own, not the Splunk connector's.
# Generate: openssl rand -hex 32
THREATQ_MCP_AUTH_TOKEN=
# A stand-in ships with the stack, since ThreatQ has no public image:
#   docker compose --profile threatq up -d soc-threatq-fake soc-mcp-threatq
#   THREATQ_BASE_URL=http://threatq:8500 with any oauth credentials
THREATQ_BASE_URL=https://threatq.local
# Looking an indicator up by value is a search: GET /api/indicators has no
# value filter (REST API Reference v5.12).
THREATQ_SEARCH_PATH=/api/indicators/search
# Score, sources and adversaries are related objects and are ABSENT unless named
# here. Without this every indicator comes back looking unscored.
THREATQ_SEARCH_WITH=score,sources,adversaries,status,type,attributes
# oauth = password grant at THREATQ_TOKEN_PATH; bearer = static THREATQ_TOKEN.
THREATQ_AUTH_MODE=oauth
THREATQ_CLIENT_ID=
THREATQ_EMAIL=
THREATQ_PASSWORD=
THREATQ_TOKEN=
THREATQ_VERIFY_SSL=true
THREATQ_TIMEOUT=60
THREATQ_MCP_PORT=8300
THREATQ_MCP_ALLOWED_HOSTS=soc-mcp-threatq:8300,localhost:8300,127.0.0.1:8300

# ========== OLLAMA ==========

# ========== LLM SERVER (agent platform) ==========
# soc-ai does not talk to Ollama; it asks the LLM server to run an agent and
# reads the answer off the stream. That server is another team's, so nothing in
# soc-ai assumes its shape — these settings are how it adapts.
# {agent} is substituted. A server that takes the agent in the body instead can
# use a static path here and name the body field in LLM_AGENT_FIELD.
# soc-analyst, not soc-triage: it is the one that delegates to
# splunk-query. soc-triage holds the Splunk tools itself and so skips
# the two-agent split entirely — pointing here was why no production
# run ever produced a delegate step.
# Body field names — the part that differs most between implementations.
# Extra body fields as JSON, e.g. {"stream": true}
# auto = follow the response content type; always = treat the body as a stream
# whatever it says; never = expect one JSON object.
# If their service wants a bearer token / other headers (JSON).
# An agent run makes several model and tool calls.

# ========== ENRICHMENT (runs in the backend, before the model) ==========
# The worker extracts indicators from the alert and checks them against ThreatQ
# before any agent runs. Both are exact work with one right answer, so they are
# not left for a model to decide whether to bother with.
#
# The ThreatQ gateway's HTTP lookup route. Blank disables enrichment, and the
# summary then says the check did not happen rather than implying it was clean.
# One alert can carry dozens of addresses; checking all of them costs the
# platform more than the answer is worth.
# Which index holds the logs for the device that raised an alert. The SOAR
# already names the device, so the agent's first search is a lookup rather than
# something it has to work out — and a wrong guess there is what makes it report
# "nothing found" for data that is one index away.
#
# Rules are pattern=index[:sourcetype], matched against the alert name and the
# raw log, case-insensitively. First match wins, so put specific rules first.
# Addresses and domains that are ours. An internal value is not worth a lookup,
# and a "no record" reply for one is exactly what gets misread as "clean".
# Comma-separated; a leading dot matches any subdomain.

# ========== JWT ==========

# ========== APP ==========

# ========== DEFAULT ADMIN (change after first login) ==========

# ========== RETENTION ==========

# ========== CORS ==========

# ── The Splunk stage ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# manual = stage 1 stores what it needs and stops; someone presses the button.
# auto   = the lookup is queued as soon as the suggestion is ready.
# Each requirement is a full agent run, which on CPU-only inference is minutes.
# The agent that writes the suggestion and lists what it would want from Splunk,
# in one call and with no tools of its own.

# The Splunk connector, for searches the backend runs itself without a model.

# ── Confluence playbooks ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Which runbook covers an alert. Leave CONFLUENCE_BASE_URL empty to run from
# mcp-server/Confluence/tests/playbooks.sample.json instead of a real space.
CONFLUENCE_MCP_AUTH_TOKEN=generate-with-openssl-rand-hex-32
CONFLUENCE_BASE_URL=
CONFLUENCE_SPACE_KEY=
CONFLUENCE_EMAIL=
CONFLUENCE_API_TOKEN=
PLAYBOOK_FILE=/playbooks/playbooks.sample.json
# Reading that file is off unless you say so. Without Confluence configured and
# with this false, the connector refuses to start rather than answering /match
# from playbooks that were written to exercise the matcher — an analyst cannot
# tell one of those from a real runbook by looking at it.
ALLOW_SAMPLE_PLAYBOOKS=false
